 1         III.     SUPPORT FOR SETTLED DEPRECIATION RATES AND
 2                                 SERVICE LIVES

 3   Q.   What are the criteria that the Commission has established to review a

 4        settlement?

 5   A.   As discussed in more detail by SPS witness Zoë E. Lees, the Commission has

 6        established the following criteria for reviewing the reasonableness of a stipulation:

 7            (1)     Is the settlement a product of serious bargaining among capable,
 8                    knowledgeable parties?

 9            (2)     Does the settlement, as a whole, benefit customers and the public
10                    interest?

11            (3)     Does the settlement, as a whole, violate any important regulatory
12                    principle or practice?

13   Q.   Do the depreciation-related portions of the Stipulation satisfy these three

14        criteria?

15   A.   Yes. Ms. Lees discusses the first criterion in her testimony. In my testimony, I

16        explain that the depreciation rates and service lives provided in Section 1.2 of the

17        Stipulation satisfy items (2) and (3) of the settlement criteria.

18   Q.   Please describe Section 1.2 of the Stipulation.

19   A.   Section 1.2 provides agreed depreciation rates and service-life extensions for

20        certain generating assets. It references Exhibit C, which provides SPS’s agreed


                                                 3
                                     Case No. 25-00079-UT
                                    Testimony in Support of
                                   Comprehensive Stipulation
                                              of
                                     Michele A. Kietzman

 1        depreciation rates. As noted in the text of Section 1.2, the agreed-upon depreciation-

 2        rate changes have a net $0 impact on the stipulated revenue deficiency. Section 1.2

 3        also states that the Signatories agree to the following service life extensions:

 4             a) Extend the service life of Jones Unit 1 from 2031 to 2045;
 5             b) Extend the service life of Jones Unit 2 from 2034 to 2045;
 6             c) Extend the service life of Nichols Unit 1 from 2028 to 2035;
 7             d) Extend the service life of Nichols Unit 2 from 2027 to 2036;
 8             e) Extend the service life of Nichols Unit 3 from 2030 to 2042;
 9             f) Extend the service life of Maddox Unit 1 from 2028 to 2038;
10             g) Extend the service life of Maddox Unit 2 from 2025 to 2043;
11             h) Extend the service life of Plant X Unit 4 from 2027 to 2039; and
12             i) Extend the service lives of Tolk Units 1 and 2 from December
13                2028 to March 2029.

14   Q.   What does it mean that the agreed-upon depreciation rates have a net $0

15        impact on the stipulated revenue deficiency?

16   A.   The depreciation rates included in Exhibit C, when applied to the test year, result

17        in no change to depreciation expense compared to depreciation expense in the test

18        year using previously approved rates. When included in the revenue requirement,

19        this results in no change to the revenue deficiency.

20   Q.   Are the terms of Section 1.2 beneficial to SPS’s customers and in the public

21        interest?

22   A.   Yes. The Signatories’ agreement on depreciation expense arises from

23        recommendations by several parties, who advocated for reductions in SPS’s
                                                4
                                     Case No. 25-00079-UT
                                    Testimony in Support of
                                   Comprehensive Stipulation
                                              of
                                     Michele A. Kietzman

 1        proposed expense rates based on their assertion that lower depreciation expense

 2        would mitigate the impact of SPS’s proposed rate increase.             The stipulated

 3        depreciation rates are in the public interest because they allow SPS to recover its

 4        investments in the generation and transmission assets that are used to serve New

 5        Mexico customers, while helping to mitigate the impact to customers.

 6               Section 1.2 also benefits customers and serves the public interest because it

 7        includes agreed depreciation rates based on extended service lives for certain

 8        generating assets. Extending the service lives for these generating assets aligns cost

 9        recovery with the useful lives of the assets.

10   Q.   Does Section 1.2 violate any important regulatory principle or practice?

11   A.   No. Section 1.2 is consistent with existing regulatory principles and practices and

12        does not require any exception from a Commission rule or practice. It aligns

13        depreciation rates with agreed service lives and reflects those rates in the stipulated

14        revenue requirement.

15   Q.   Does this conclude your pre-filed testimony in support of the Stipulation?

16   A.   Yes.




